# Conclusion

This notebook demonstrated usage of the Mitelman BigQuery dataset, which includes CytoConverter chromosomal coordinate data, in combination with TCGA BigQuery tables for a comparative analysis. Specifically, the notebook computes correlation (Pearson) coefficients between gains and losses obtained with Mitelam DB and TCGA datasets.

We observed that the mayority (but not all) of the significan correlation shown in Denomy et al. paper (Table 1, https://doi.org/10.1158/0008-5472.CAN-19-0585) are also significan in this analysis.
The table structure information is ({database name: {schema name: [table name]}}): 
{'mitelman-db': {'prod': ['AuthorReference', 'CytoConvertedLog', 'KaryBreak', 'CytoConverted', 'KaryBit', 'Koder', 'RecurrentNumData', 'CytoBands_hg38', 'KaryClone', 'Cytogen', 'MolClinGene', 'RecurrentData', 'KaryAbnorm', 'MolClinBreak', 'Reference', 'MolClinAbnorm', 'CytogenInv', 'MolBiolClinAssoc', 'CytogenInvValid']}}
Begin Exploring Related Columns
Below is a quick “road‑map” of the columns that may be useful, followed by ten illustrative BigQuery SQL snippets (from the very simplest “show me a few rows” to a complete correlation example).  
Only tables contained in mitelman‑db.prod are touched, per the constraints.

Relevant columns and their possible usage
• `Cytogen.RefNo`, `Cytogen.CaseNo` –– identify the cases we want to study and let us filter on morphology / topology (`Morph = '3111'`, `Topo = '0401'`).  
• `CytoConverted.RefNo … CellsPresent` –– holds the copy‑number segments already mapped to hg38; `Chr`, `Start`, `End`, and especially `Type` (‘Gain’, ‘Loss’, ‘Amp’, etc.) are what we need for CNA tallies.  
• `CytoBands_hg38.chromosome`, `cytoband_name`, `hg38_start`, `hg38_stop` –– give boundaries for cytobands if we want finer‑grained frequencies.  
• `RecurrentNumData` –– whole‑database recurrence information that can be used as a “comparison” data set (standing‑in for TCGA in this sand‑boxed exercise).  
• `CORR( x, y )` & `isb‐cgc‐bq.functions.corr_pvalue_current( corr, n )` –– BQ functions to obtain Pearson r and its p‑value once we have vectors of frequencies.  
Conditions we will repeatedly use  
```txt
WHERE c.Morph = '3111'           -- breast carcinoma
  AND c.Topo  = '0401'           -- breast site
  AND conv.Type IN ('Gain','Amp','Loss','Deletion')
```  

Ten illustrative queries
(they are numbered for convenience; each finishes with `LIMIT 20` so they remain “lightweight”)

```sql
-- 1. Peek at CytoConverted to see the available CNA ‘Type’ values
SELECT DISTINCT `Type`
FROM   `mitelman-db.prod.CytoConverted`
LIMIT  20;
```

```sql
-- 2. How many Mitelman breast‑cancer cases exist (Morph 3111 / Topo 0401)?
SELECT COUNT(DISTINCT CONCAT(RefNo,'-',CaseNo)) AS breast_cases
FROM   `mitelman-db.prod.Cytogen`
WHERE  Morph = '3111'
  AND  Topo  = '0401'
LIMIT  20;
```

```sql
-- 3. Join Cytogen → CytoConverted and list a few gain/loss rows for QA
SELECT conv.RefNo, conv.CaseNo, conv.Chr, conv.Start, conv.End, conv.Type
FROM   `mitelman-db.prod.Cytogen`      AS c
JOIN   `mitelman-db.prod.CytoConverted` AS conv
  ON   c.RefNo  = conv.RefNo
 AND   c.CaseNo = conv.CaseNo
WHERE  c.Morph  = '3111'
  AND  c.Topo   = '0401'
  AND  conv.Type IN ('Gain','Loss','Amp','Deletion')
LIMIT  20;
```

```sql
-- 4. Distinct chromosomes represented in the filtered cohort
SELECT DISTINCT conv.Chr
FROM   `mitelman-db.prod.Cytogen`      AS c
JOIN   `mitelman-db.prod.CytoConverted` AS conv
  ON   c.RefNo  = conv.RefNo
 AND   c.CaseNo = conv.CaseNo
WHERE  c.Morph = '3111'
  AND  c.Topo  = '0401'
LIMIT  20;
```

```sql
-- 5. Count #gain segments per chromosome (breast cohort)
SELECT conv.Chr,
       COUNT(*) AS gains
FROM   `mitelman-db.prod.Cytogen`      AS c
JOIN   `mitelman-db.prod.CytoConverted` AS conv
  ON   c.RefNo  = conv.RefNo
 AND   c.CaseNo = conv.CaseNo
WHERE  c.Morph = '3111'
  AND  c.Topo  = '0401'
  AND  conv.Type IN ('Gain','Amp')
GROUP  BY conv.Chr
ORDER  BY gains DESC
LIMIT  20;
```

```sql
-- 6. Count #loss/deletion segments per chromosome (breast cohort)
SELECT conv.Chr,
       COUNT(*) AS losses
FROM   `mitelman-db.prod.Cytogen`      AS c
JOIN   `mitelman-db.prod.CytoConverted` AS conv
  ON   c.RefNo  = conv.RefNo
 AND   c.CaseNo = conv.CaseNo
WHERE  c.Morph = '3111'
  AND  c.Topo  = '0401'
  AND  conv.Type IN ('Loss','Deletion')
GROUP  BY conv.Chr
ORDER  BY losses DESC
LIMIT  20;
```

```sql
-- 7. Prepared reference: total Mitelman gain counts (all morph/topo) per chromosome
SELECT Chr,
       COUNT(*) AS mitelman_all_gains
FROM   `mitelman-db.prod.CytoConverted`
WHERE  `Type` IN ('Gain','Amp')
GROUP BY Chr
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8. Combine cohort‑specific and all‑database gain counts side‑by‑side
WITH brca AS (
  SELECT Chr, COUNT(*) AS brca_gains
  FROM   `mitelman-db.prod.Cytogen`      AS c
  JOIN   `mitelman-db.prod.CytoConverted` AS conv
    ON   c.RefNo  = conv.RefNo
   AND   c.CaseNo = conv.CaseNo
  WHERE  c.Morph = '3111'
    AND  c.Topo  = '0401'
    AND  conv.Type IN ('Gain','Amp')
  GROUP BY Chr
),
all_mitel AS (
  SELECT Chr, COUNT(*) AS all_gains
  FROM   `mitelman-db.prod.CytoConverted`
  WHERE  `Type` IN ('Gain','Amp')
  GROUP BY Chr
)
SELECT a.Chr,
       brca_gains,
       all_gains
FROM   brca AS a
JOIN   all_mitel USING (Chr)
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9. Pearson correlation for chromosome‑level gains
--    (Mitelman breast cohort vs whole‑database counts)
WITH brca AS (
  SELECT Chr, COUNT(*) AS brca_gains
  FROM   `mitelman-db.prod.Cytogen`      AS c
  JOIN   `mitelman-db.prod.CytoConverted` AS conv
    ON   c.RefNo  = conv.RefNo
   AND   c.CaseNo = conv.CaseNo
  WHERE  c.Morph = '3111'
    AND  c.Topo  = '0401'
    AND  conv.Type IN ('Gain','Amp')
  GROUP BY Chr
),
all_mitel AS (
  SELECT Chr, COUNT(*) AS all_gains
  FROM   `mitelman-db.prod.CytoConverted`
  WHERE  `Type` IN ('Gain','Amp')
  GROUP BY Chr
),
merged AS (
  SELECT b.Chr,
         b.brca_gains AS x,
         a.all_gains  AS y
  FROM   all_mitel AS a
  JOIN   brca      AS b USING (Chr)
)
SELECT
  CORR(x, y)                                         AS pearson_r,
  isb-cgc-bq.functions.corr_pvalue_current(CORR(x,y), COUNT(*)) AS p_value,
  COUNT(*)                                           AS n_chromosomes
FROM merged
HAVING n_chromosomes >= 5
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10. End‑to‑end example that
--     a) produces gain, loss, amp, deletion frequencies for breast cohort
--     b) joins to RecurrentNumData as a surrogate “external” data set
--     c) computes per‑aberration Pearson correlations (filter n>=5)
WITH brca AS (
  SELECT conv.Chr         AS chromosome,
         conv.Type        AS aberration_type,
         COUNT(*)         AS brca_ct
  FROM   `mitelman-db.prod.Cytogen`      AS cy
  JOIN   `mitelman-db.prod.CytoConverted` AS conv
    ON   cy.RefNo  = conv.RefNo
   AND   cy.CaseNo = conv.CaseNo
  WHERE  cy.Morph = '3111'
    AND  cy.Topo  = '0401'
    AND  conv.Type IN ('Gain','Amp','Loss','Deletion')
  GROUP  BY chromosome, aberration_type
),
rec AS (
  -- RecurrentNumData holds total‑case counts for each (chromosome, abnormality)
  -- Map its 'Abnormality' prefix to a generic aberration type
  SELECT chromosome             AS chromosome,
         CASE
           WHEN LEFT(Abnormality,1) = '+' THEN 'Gain'
           WHEN LEFT(Abnormality,1) = '-' THEN 'Loss'
           ELSE 'Other'
         END                    AS aberration_type,
         SUM(CAST(TotalCases AS INT64)) AS rec_ct
  FROM   `mitelman-db.prod.RecurrentNumData`
  GROUP  BY chromosome, aberration_type
),
joined AS (
  SELECT b.chromosome,
         b.aberration_type,
         b.brca_ct        AS x,
         r.rec_ct         AS y
  FROM   brca AS b
  JOIN   rec  AS r
    ON   b.chromosome       = r.chromosome
   AND   b.aberration_type  = r.aberration_type
)
SELECT aberration_type,
       CORR(x, y)  AS pearson_r,
       isb-cgc-bq.functions.corr_pvalue_current(CORR(x,y), COUNT(*)) AS p_value,
       COUNT(*)    AS n_chromosomes
FROM   joined
GROUP  BY aberration_type
HAVING n_chromosomes >= 5
LIMIT 20;
```

These ten queries walk from a quick reconnaissance of the data all the way up to a full per‑aberration Pearson correlation.  
The final snippet illustrates how you could swap in any external TCGA frequency table (same two fields: chromosome & count) in place of the `rec` CTE, while still satisfying the ≥5‑items requirement for each correlation calculation.
